```text
9. testing — 'all clear, proceed' gate
    test the backend in-process, no server, no browser, no real disk.
    seams already exist: DiskPort + ConfigParserPort are ABCs, so fake them.
    what to cover, in risk order:
      - ingestion / numap entity graph (validators, dep ordering, ingest regex)
      - kb state machine (handle_start, domain select, prompt select, hot path)
      - render pipeline (hydration, truncation, hot-prompt resolver, manifest)
      - apply_file_changes (block parsing, deletelist, fence stripping)
    shape:
      - one pytest file or small tests/ dir
      - fixture factories: in-memory disk (dict of path->content), fake cfg parser, canonical workspace
      - fake disk records writes/deletes for assertion
      - the gate is just: run pytest, exit 0 = green light
    out of scope:
      - http server wiring (thin delegation)
      - frontend (display layer)
      - gpu box proxy (pass-through)
```
```text
11. provider model:
    Clanker's system config can declare providers.
    The auth tokens and such would be carried in another file, contained in clanker repo but .gitignored. needs to be safe, anywayy

    a. commercial providers
       - anything that speaks /v1/chat/completions (this is openai's api?)
       - user supplies base_url + api_key (or no key for local)
       - this is the "default" shape, most providers fit here

    b. gpu-box (custom)
       - the tailnet-hosted thing, local GPU
       - adds host-side control: desktop/inference mode switching, raw box management

    c. noprovider public browser gui llm offerings on the free tier
       - basically the user will use the webui to get prompts to clipboard
       - paste those into webui of providers, converse
       - copy replies which are intented for ingestion over to clank webui, paste
```

III. Items under development

Backlog tail, Selected item/items:

It's a frontend-only change. The controller's `proxy_gpu` streams the raw SSE response straight through to the browser (see `clank-server.py` `_write` → `HTTPResponse` branch), so the GPU box's `prompt_progress` events already reach the client. The only gap is that `streamChatCompletion` in `api-client.js` parses each SSE line but ignores `.prompt_progress`.

Recommended surface: a progress bar on the **assistant** message, shown on first `prompt_progress` and hidden the instant the first content delta arrives. Keep it on the assistant message (not the collapsed user prompt) so it stays visible during prefill.

```
¤ backlog.backlog ¤
Backlog tail, Selected item/items:

Surface GPU-box prefill progress to the user during prompt generation. The SSE `prompt_progress` events already stream through the controller's proxy_gpu pass-through to the browser; the frontend must surface them instead of ignoring them.

    [ ] 'content/web-ui/frontend/api-client.js': Forward `prompt_progress` SSE events via a new `onPrefill` callback.
        - Destructure `onPrefill` from the `streamChatCompletion` options.
        - In `processLine`, add a branch before the reasoning/content branches: `if (json.prompt_progress) { onPrefill?.(json.prompt_progress); }` using fields `processed`, `total`, `time_ms`, `cache`.
        - Prohibit: do not drop or coalesce `prompt_progress` events — every tick must be forwarded.

    [ ] 'content/web-ui/frontend/message.js': Add a prefill progress bar shown during prefill and hidden once content begins.
        - Add a `.prefill-bar` element to the assistant message (independent of the collapsed toggle).
        - On first `onPrefill`, show the bar and set width = `processed/total`; render percentage and elapsed seconds.
        - Compute a rolling tokens/sec rate from consecutive ticks: `(processed_now − processed_prev) / ((time_ms_now − time_ms_prev) / 1000)`. Display it next to the percentage once a prior tick exists; on the very first tick (no prior) skip the rate or fall back to `processed / (time_ms / 1000)`.
        - Hide the bar when the first content token arrives or when the message completes.
        - Prohibit: do not let the bar persist into the rendered reply.

    [ ] 'content/web-ui/frontend/clanker-app.js': Wire `onPrefill` into the `streamChatCompletion` call, targeting the assistant message.
        - Pass `onPrefill: (pp) => assistantMsg.onPrefill(pp)`.
        - Prohibit: do not couple prefill feedback to the collapsed user/prompt message.

Backlog tail end

VI. finished stuffs

Prefill prompts from keyboard bar with max_tokens=1 and collapsed prompt message

    [x] 'content/web-ui/frontend/clanker-app.js': Route the `prefill` flag from the `prompt-fired` event into `#generateResponse` and thread it to the API call.
        - Add an options argument `{ prefill = false } = {}` to `#generateResponse(prompt, options = {})`.
        - In the `prompt-fired` listener, call `this.#generateResponse(e.detail, { prefill: true });`
        - In the `submit` listener, call `this.#generateResponse(prompt);` (no options, unchanged).
        - Create the user/prompt message, set `userMsg.collapsed = true` when `prefill` is true.
        - Pass `max_tokens: prefill ? 1 : undefined` into the `streamChatCompletion` call.

    [x] 'content/web-ui/frontend/message.js': Add a `collapsed` flag honored at instantiation.
        - Add `collapsed = false;` as a class field on `Message`.
        - In `onConnect`, after `this.#renderContent()`, if `this.collapsed` is true, call `this.contentElement?.removeAttribute('open');`
        - Prohibit: do not gate the assistant message on this flag — only the prompt/user message is collapsed.

    [x] 'content/web-ui/frontend/api-client.js': Forward `max_tokens` to the backend only when provided.
        - Destructure `max_tokens` from the `streamChatCompletion` options.
        - In the request body, spread `...(max_tokens !== undefined ? { max_tokens: max_tokens } : {})`.
        - Prohibit: always sending `max_tokens` — it must be omitted for normal (non-prefill) prompts.